<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=trireme">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trireme</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as trieres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The trireme was an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> galley driven by three banks of oars with one man to each oar, first referred to at the end of the 8th century BCE; scholars are divided on whether it came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. Fast and agile, with a crew of about 200 including 170 rowers, it was the dominant warship of the Mediterranean from the 7th to the 4th centuries BCE. Triremes were of great importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>, and a fleet of over 200 was the foundation of Athenian power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trireme.jpg" alt="Trireme" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Da jackson · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Olympias%22,_Nachbau_einer_Triere_im_Schiffsmuseum_Trokadero_Marina,_Paleo_Faliro,_Athen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The trireme developed from the penteconter, a single-banked warship with 25 oars on each side, and from the bireme, a two-banked warship of Phoenician origin. Biremes are common on vases and pottery fragments from the 8th century BCE, and the first references to three-banked ships come at the end of that century.</p><p>Modern scholars are divided on whether the trireme came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. Thucydides says the Corinthians introduced it to Greece in the late 8th century BCE, and Pliny and Diodorus took this to mean that it was invented at Corinth. Clement of Alexandria attributes it to the Sidonians. The first definite reference to triremes in battle is of about 525 BCE, when Polycrates of Samos contributed 40 to a Persian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. By 490 BCE they were the backbone of the Persian fleet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Excavated ship sheds at Zea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, the main war harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, were about 40 metres long and 6 metres wide, which with Vitruvius's allowance for each rower suggests a ship just under 37 metres long. Its draught was about 1 metre, and it could be beached easily. The ship had 170 oars, one man to each, two masts with square sails, and two steering oars at the stern.</p><p>The complement was about 200: the 170 rowers, a deck crew under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trierarchy">trierarch</a> and a marine detachment. Crews were free men, not galley slaves. An Athenian crew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> typically had 80 citizens, 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metic">metics</a> and 60 foreign hands. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> a wealthy trierarch paid to man and maintain the ship, which belonged to the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The trireme relied on speed and manoeuvre rather than boarding. Squadrons used the periplous, outflanking the enemy to strike the rear, and the diekplous, a charge to break the enemy line. In 483/2 BCE Themistocles persuaded the Athenian assembly to build 200 triremes with the income of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a> silver mines. The Persian fleet was defeated at Salamis, after a first clash at Artemisium.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> larger warships, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a>, replaced it as ramming grew less effective against armoured bows. Between 1985 and 1987 a trireme named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> on the advice of the historian J. S. Morrison and the naval architect John F. Coates. Crewed by 170 volunteers, it could turn through 180 degrees in one minute, and its trials suggest that ancient accounts of its performance were not exaggerated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trireme” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the urging of Themistocles the city then built a fleet of some 200 <b>triremes</b>, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated Xerxes I at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The mines of Crenides, refounded as Philippi in 356 BCE, paid for a permanent standing army and the kingdom's first small navy of <b>triremes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 494 BCE the Ionian fleet of 353 <b>triremes</b>, including 80 from Miletus, was defeated by the Persians at the Battle of Lade, an island just off the city, after most of the Samian squadron deserted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thetes, with metics and hired foreigners, rowed the <b>triremes</b> that won at Salamis in 480 BCE and held the empire together, and a fleet of 200 triremes needed some 34,000 oarsmen, paid for their service.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>late 8th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Greece or Phoenicia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Whether it came from Greece (Corinth, by Thucydides) or Phoenicia (Sidon) is disputed; triremes are first definitely recorded in battle about 525 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=trireme">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
